{
  "dom_id": "step-q105382",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "The gravitational force between two particles of mass ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ that are at a distance $r$ from each other is given by the formula ${F}_{\\text{G}}=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}},$ where $G$ is the gravitational constant measured in $\\frac{{\\text{m}}^{3}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}\\,\\text{kg}},$ masses ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are measured in kilograms, and the distance $r$ is measured in meters. Find an appropriate measurement unit for the gravitational force.",
  "worked_solution": "We can find the unit of gravitational force by substituting the given unit of the gravitational constant, mass, and distance into the formula and simplifying it.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{\\text{G}} & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\text{m}}^{3}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}\\,\\text{kg}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{kg}}{{\\text{m}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\text{m}}^{3}\\,{\\text{kg}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}\\,\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{m}\\cdot \\cancel{{\\text{m}}^{2}}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\cancel{\\text{kg}}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}\\,\\cancel{\\text{kg}}\\,\\cancel{{\\text{m}}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the unit of the gravitational force is $\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
